---
// src/pages/projects/mast-segmentation.astro
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout title="Cell Segmentation U-Net Model — Nicholas Grzelak">
  <article class="project-page">
    <header class="project-header">
      <p class="label">R-005 · Master's Research</p>
      <h1>Cell Segmentation U-Net Model</h1>
      <p class="project-lede">
        Cell classification and segmentation model trained on a Kaggle dataset.
      </p>
      <p class="label project-facts">
        2025 · Python · PyTorch · Machine vision · Data augmentation
      </p>
    </header>

    <!--
      Images: drop files into the /public folder of your project, e.g.
      public/projects/plotter/render-01.jpg, then reference them from the
      site root like below. Delete this placeholder block once you have
      real images.
    -->
    <figure class="project-figure">
      <img src="/projects/seg/cellimages.png" alt="Segmentation Model Results" />
      <!-- <img src="/projects/plotter/render-01.jpg" alt="Isometric CAD render of the plotter frame" /> -->
      <figcaption class="label">FIG. 1 — Left: raw cell image; middle: labelled data; right: mask predicted by the custom model</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <h2>Overview</h2>
    <p>
      This project was initially developed to test cell segmentation and verify a pipeline which would later be used in my self-driving lab work.
    </p>

    <h2>Design</h2>
    <p>
      Like many methods in the literature, I used a pre-trained U-Net before training on another 4000 images. The images were a combination of regular and augmented images,
       since the original <a href="https://www.kaggle.com/counting-cells-in-microscopy-images-2023">Kaggle dataset</a> was sparse. 65% of the 4000 images were augmented with
       rotations, flips and Gaussian blur.

       After the model generated a black and white image mask, OpenCV was used to count the number of cells within the mask.
    </p>

    <!-- <figure class="project-figure">
      <div class="img-placeholder" aria-hidden="true">
        PHOTO — ASSEMBLED MACHINE (replace with your image)
      </div>
      <figcaption class="label">FIG. 2 — Assembled machine, first plot</figcaption>
    </figure> -->

    <h2>Results</h2>
    <p>
      After 100 epochs, the model achieved a pixel accuracy of 95.83% on test data. In total, the model overpredicted the cell count by 2.27%. This model laid the foundation
       for developing an optical scanner specifically for the purpose of cell counting. While the model here was not state of the art, it proved that cells could be counted
      with reasonable accuracy using optical methods. Future work can compare this U-Net model to other models in the literature, or to other cell types and densities.
    </p>

    <p class="back-link"><a href="/">← Back to index</a></p>
  </article>
</Layout>

<style>
  .project-page {
    padding: 4rem 0 1rem;
  }

  .project-header {
    margin-bottom: 3rem;
  }

  .project-header h1 {
    font-size: clamp(1.7rem, 4vw, 2.4rem);
    margin-top: 1rem;
  }

  .project-lede {
    margin-top: 1.25rem;
    max-width: 52ch;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
  }

  .project-facts {
    margin-top: 1.25rem;
  }

  .project-page h2 {
    font-size: 1.25rem;
    font-weight: 400;
    margin: 2.5rem 0 0.75rem;
  }

  .project-figure {
    margin: 2.5rem 0;
  }

  .project-figure figcaption {
    margin-top: 0.6rem;
  }

  .img-placeholder {
    display: grid;
    place-items: center;
    aspect-ratio: 16 / 9;
    border: 1px solid var(--rule-strong);
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: 0.72rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.12em;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    text-align: center;
    padding: 1rem;
  }

  .back-link {
    margin-top: 3.5rem;
  }
</style>